"Earth has about 7.6 million superhumans; over 10.5 billion people will never get powers."
"A poll found 96% of students want powers."
"Code names are common among superheroes but not universal."
"Interesting scars are like collector's items for supers."
"Cities hire heroes-in-residence; in the Body Drainer's time Chicago had space for a pair."
"Emergency dispatch sends heroes to superhuman incidents."
"Heroes appear on the six o'clock news, with footage spliced to flatter them."
"Superhuman-hate websites and superpower enthusiast blogs both thrive."
"Few superhumans get to travel unofficially."
"Young superhumans are all over social media."
"Most countries have laws against hiring Avowed as soldiers or police."
"Military or special police usually back heroes in local work."
"Serious problems go to formal strike crews from Anesidora."
"Elite battle squads come out only for massive emergencies and demon-squashing."
"Major cities pay for neither solo sidekicks nor, much, pre-made pairs or teams."
"Some people, called dissidents, speak against the Avowed."
"'Anesidora for Anesidorans' types think non-Avowed should not be allowed on the island."
"Fairy and Flarey, an Australian cartoon, stars two unique-class S-ranks."
"A Children's Encyclopedia of the Avowed interviews real superhumans in every chapter."
"US mail to Anesidora is slow."
"Avowed are a very small percentage of humanity."
"Good Avowed are expected to stay on the island; Avowed on other resource worlds form communities too."
"Conspiracy theorists say Avowed are gathered on Anesidora so they can be nuked."
"Avowed stratify by class and rank; high ranks are the top fifteen percent of young Avowed."
"Superhuman path etiquette: the faster yields, and super speed keeps to the far left."
"Avowed exclaim 'By my powers.'"
"Every major city has a pretty little mascot hero team."
"Hero culture is half about the hero part and half about the super part, and is celebrity-driven."
"Anesidora exists partly because normal humans want to limit Avowed access to the species."
"The U.S. allows visiting Avowed fourteen days of family time a year, booked up to 18 months ahead."
"Visiting Avowed need school, country and monitoring hero team permission, and sign arrest waivers."
"Anesidora lets residents book visitors two years ahead; holiday slots fill at once."
"Human would-be wizards obsess over Artonans; some wear fake contract tattoos."
"A couple of wizards have married humans."
"Superhuman sporting events and performances sell out in seconds."
"Human performing arts are catching on on the Triplanets; Avowed dancers get summoned to perform."
"Earth's healthcare is Triplanet-gifted; some vaccines and medicines come through the Contract."
"Getting Contract vaccines means signing a paper agreeing to the Contract's terms."
"Genetically engineered children are illegal in many countries; magical wombs are widely allowed."
"Artonans pay South American countries not to develop the Amazon."
"Artonans see Anesidora, confining magic users to one country, as a human idiosyncrasy."
"Anesidorans call Avowed from the rest of Earth globies, fondly or angrily."
"Third- and fourth-generation Anesidorans have no relatives abroad to visit."
"It is illegal on Earth for Avowed to live among average humans; heroes capture unregistered ones."
"Superheroing is half fighting harm-doers and half entertainment, called battle theater."
"Above an active-duty hero come only military action, a hyperbole, or an Anesidoran battle group."
"Unwritten transit etiquette gives seats first to non-Avowed, who wear a non-Avowed tag."
"On Anesidora the gray recycling bin is for items with possible magical residue."
